---
// Review page for reader levels. Routed at /dev/levels/ in `pnpm dev` only.
// Try ?level=technical, #deeper-why-counts-differ-between-models, find-in-page on
// "tokenizer", printing, and switching levels halfway down the page.
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Deeper from '../components/level/Deeper.astro';
import ForLevel from '../components/level/ForLevel.astro';
import { Content as Example } from './levels-example.md';

const test = `import { describe, expect, it } from 'vitest';
import { openingHours } from './hours';

describe('opening hours on public holidays', () => {
  it('shows the holiday hours on Easter Monday', () => {
    const day = new Date('2027-03-29T09:00:00');
    expect(openingHours(day)).toEqual({ open: '09:00', close: '13:00' });
  });

  it('keeps the normal hours the day after', () => {
    const day = new Date('2027-03-30T09:00:00');
    expect(openingHours(day)).toEqual({ open: '07:00', close: '18:00' });
  });
});`;
---
<Base title="Reader levels" description="Review page for the reader-level blocks, picker and offer." noindex>
  <article class="lv">
    <h1>Reader levels</h1>
    <p class="lv-status" aria-live="off">
      Showing the <b data-lv-now>plain</b> level. Last <code>acg:level</code> event: <b data-lv-event>none yet</b>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="what-the-agent-reads">What the agent reads</h2>
    <p>
      When you ask an agent to change the club list, it does not see your screen. It reads your request, the files it opens,
      and the replies from the commands it runs. All of that has to fit in its working memory, called the context window, and
      anything outside it does not exist for the agent.
    </p>
    <p>
      So the most useful thing you can do is tell it where to look: name the page, the file, or the thing that looks wrong.
      See also <a href="#deeper-why-counts-differ-between-models">why token counts differ</a>, which sits two layers down.
    </p>

    <Deeper level="technical" title="What a token is">
      <p>
        Models read text in tokens, pieces of words a few characters long. A short English word is usually one token; a long
        or rare word is several. Code tends to cost more tokens than prose of the same length, because of symbols and
        indentation.
      </p>
      <p>
        The context window is measured in tokens, and so is what you pay on an API key. When an agent reads a large file it
        spends tokens, whether or not the file turns out to matter.
      </p>
      <Deeper level="engineer" title="Why counts differ between models">
        <p>
          Each maker trains its own tokenizer, the table that splits text into tokens. The same file can come out at noticeably
          different counts under two models, so a context size or a per-token price only compares cleanly within one maker.
          When you compare costs across makers, compare the cost of finishing the same task.
        </p>
      </Deeper>
    </Deeper>

    <h2 id="checking-the-fix">Checking the fix</h2>
    <p>
      The bakery's site shows the normal opening hours on public holidays. The agent says it has fixed it. Its summary is its
      own account of what it did; whether the page is right is something you check.
    </p>
    <ForLevel is="plain">
      <p class="lv-path">
        Your path: ask the agent to show you the page as it would look on the next public holiday, with a screenshot, and
        check the hours against the sign in the shop window.
      </p>
    </ForLevel>
    <ForLevel is={['technical', 'engineer']}>
      <p class="lv-path">
        Your path: ask for a test that pins the date, run it yourself, and read the diff before you merge.
      </p>
    </ForLevel>

    <Deeper level="technical" title="A test that pins the date">
      <p>
        A test for this bug has to fix the date, or it only passes on the days you happen to run it. Ask the agent for two
        cases, the holiday and the day after, so a fix that breaks normal days is caught too.
      </p>
      <Code code={test} lang="ts" themes={{ light: 'github-light', dark: 'github-dark' }} />
      <div class="lv-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Day</th><th>Expected</th><th>Shown before the fix</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>29 March 2027</td><td>Easter Monday</td><td>09:00 to 13:00</td><td>07:00 to 18:00</td></tr>
            <tr><td>30 March 2027</td><td>Tuesday</td><td>07:00 to 18:00</td><td>07:00 to 18:00</td></tr>
            <tr><td>25 December 2027</td><td>Christmas Day</td><td>Closed</td><td>07:00 to 18:00</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </Deeper>

    <Deeper level="engineer" title="Where the holiday list should live" id="holiday-list">
      <p>
        The tempting fix is a hard-coded list of dates in the hours function. It passes the test and goes stale the following
        year. Ask where holidays come from instead: a maintained calendar package, a feed, or a small data file someone owns
        and updates once a year.
      </p>
      <p>
        Whichever it is, keep the lookup at the edge. The hours function should take the holiday calendar as an argument, so
        the test can hand it a fixed calendar and production can hand it the real one. That also makes the summary easier to
        check: the diff touching the calendar source is a different kind of change from the diff touching the hours logic,
        and a reviewer should see them separately.
      </p>
      <p>
        Watch for time zones. A server in another zone renders the page for a date that is already tomorrow, or still
        yesterday, for the shop. Build the date from the shop's own zone before you look it up.
      </p>
      <p>
        Finally, ask the agent what it did not check. A good answer names the cases it skipped, such as holidays that move each
        year or a holiday falling on a Sunday. A summary that claims everything is covered is the one to read most carefully.
      </p>
    </Deeper>

    <section class="lv-md">
      <Example />
    </section>
  </article>
</Base>

<script>
  import { getLevel, onLevel } from '../lib/levels';
  const now = document.querySelector('[data-lv-now]');
  const last = document.querySelector('[data-lv-event]');
  if (now) now.textContent = getLevel();
  onLevel((level) => {
    if (now) now.textContent = level;
    if (last) last.textContent = level;
  });
</script>

<style>
  .lv { max-width: var(--measure); margin: 2.5rem auto 0; padding: 0 var(--gutter); }
  .lv h1 { font-size: 2.2rem; line-height: 1.15; margin: 0 0 0.75rem; }
  .lv :global(h2) { font-size: 1.5rem; line-height: 1.25; margin: 2.75rem 0 0.75rem; }
  .lv-status { color: var(--muted); font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.875rem; }
  .lv-path { padding-left: 1rem; border-left: 2px solid var(--accent); }
  .lv-scroll { overflow-x: auto; margin: 1rem 0; }
  .lv table { border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 0.9375rem; min-width: 30rem; }
  .lv th, .lv td { text-align: left; padding: 0.4rem 0.75rem 0.4rem 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); }
  .lv :global(blockquote) { margin: 1.5rem 0; padding-left: 1rem; border-left: 2px solid var(--rule); color: var(--ink-2); font-style: italic; }
</style>
